2026-06-30 15:48:55,071 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-b7c80da0-0912-4b48-b2f4-1bfce4e8a553', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: sex\nsdrf_column: characteristics[sex]\nontology: PATO:0000047\ndescription: Biological sex of the donor.\nallowed_values: male, female, intersex, hermaphrodite, anonymized, not available, not applicable\ninstructions: Use one of the fixed values above. Return "not available" if not stated.\n  Return "not applicable" if the sample is from a cell line or organism without defined sex.\n\nterm: age\nsdrf_column: characteristics[age]\nontology: EFO:0000246\ndescription: Age of the donor at sample collection.\npattern: {Number}{Unit} where Unit is Y (years), M (months), W (weeks), D (days).\n  Also allowed: "anonymized" if age is stated but anonymized.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: ancestry category\nsdrf_column: characteristics[ancestry category]\nontology: HANCESTRO\ndescription: Ancestry or ethnic background of the donor.\ninstructions: Use HANCESTRO terms if possible. Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: biological replicate\nsdrf_column: characteristics[biological replicate]\nontology: MS:1001809\ndescription: Identifier for the biological replicate (integer or "pooled").\ninstructions: Return a description of the replication strategy from the paper\n  (e.g. "3 biological replicates", "2 independent experiments"). The actual\n  integer values are assigned per-file automatically; describe what you find in the text.\n\nterm: genotype\nsdrf_column: characteristics[genotype]\nontology: EFO:0000513\ndescription: Known genetic variant, mutation, or genotype of the subject.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Example: "wild type", "Δatg5", "ΔYme1".\n\nterm: pooled sample\nsdrf_column: characteristics[pooled sample]\nontology: NCIT:C165587\ndescription: Whether the sample is a pooled sample.\nallowed_values: not pooled, pooled, or pattern SN=sample1;SN=sample2\ninstructions: Return "not pooled" unless explicitly stated otherwise.\n\nterm: phosphate\nsdrf_column: characteristics[phosphate]\nontology: PRIDE:0000805\ndescription: Phosphate concentration (only relevant for environmental/water samples).\npattern: number + mg/L or umol/L\ninstructions: Return null unless this is a water/environmental sample with explicit\n  phosphate concentration stated.\n\nterm: temperature\nsdrf_column: characteristics[temperature]\nontology: PRIDE:0000703\ndescription: Temperature at the sampling location (environmental samples only).\npattern: number + °C\ninstructions: Return null unless this is an environmental/metaproteomics sample\n  with explicit temperature stated. Do NOT confuse with crosslinking temperature.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "[TITLE]\nDexter Energy Transfer Photocatalysis Enables Covalent Label-ing and Profiling of Amyloid Plagues in Alzheimer’s Disease Tissue\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nAmyloid plaque is a pathological hallmark in Alzheimer's disease (AD) brain with their composition associated with dis-ease etiology. Here, we report a photocatalyst, namely AmyCAT, that selectively binds to amyloid fibrils in AD brain tis-sue and undergoes Dexter energy transfer (DET) to catalyze carbene production for amyloid proximity labeling. First, we design the AmyCAT photocatalyst to generally bind different types of amyloids. Second, we energetically match the DET effect in a 12 × 4 photocatalysts-substrates array to activate diazo substrate into reactive carbene by visible green light (530~545 nm). Further, we demonstrate that the optimal AmyCAT probe catalyzes pan-amyloid protein labeling with 30-fold selectivity over folded counterparts. Mechanistic studies confirm the photocatalytic labeling is indeed via DET pro-cess, which tailors the proximity effect for spatially confined amyloid labeling. Finally, we employ the AmyCAT photo-catalyst to label, enrich and profile amyloid plagues in AD brain tissue, revealing key proteins and pathways associated with AD pathological deposition and etiology.\n\n[METHODS]\nThe tryptic peptides were dissolved in solvent A, directly loaded onto a home-made reversed-phase analytical column  (25-cm length, 100 μm i.d.). The mobile phase consisted of solvent A (0.1% formic acid, 2% acetonitrile/in water) and  solvent B (0.1% formic acid, 90% acetonitrile/in water). Peptides were separated with the following gradient: 0-22.5  min，6%-22%B；22.5-26.5 min，22%-34%B；26.5-28.5 min，34%-80%B；28.5-30 min，80%B, and all at a constant  flow rate of 700 nl/min on a EASY-nLC 1200 UPLC system (ThermoFisher Scientific). The separated peptides were  analyzed in Orbitrap Exploris 480 with a nano-electrospray ion source. The electrospray voltage applied was 2300 V.  FAIMS compensate voltage(CV) was set as-45 V. Precursors and fragments were analyzed at the Orbitrap detector.  The full MS scan resolution was set to 60000 for a scan range of 350-1400 m/z.  The MS/MS scan was fixed first mass  as 120.0 m/z at a resolution of 15000. The HCD fragmentation was performed at a normalized collision energy (NCE)  of 27 %. Automatic gain control (AGC) target was set at 1E6, with a maximum injection time of 22 ms.\n"}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
